**Explicit adjudications, including incorrect auditor claims:**

- **G/RT/AP/C are wrong where they infer frozen throughput, monotonically increasing power, phase=segment age, or Φ-trained C3-S BASE.** Current path loss/interference/fading remain active; authenticated repriced heads contain no Φ. Fewer handovers would not refute selective renewal.
- **G’s occupancy-factor energy “deflation,” unsourced hardware totals, universal SE ceiling and claimed definitive attribution are unsupported.** Charging one shared PA once is correct; H02 is the separate transmission inconsistency.
- **AP’s corrected 6.266–8.721 W range is still not a universal bound:** its own audit reports powers below p⁰. Its 0.744-dB example concerns an isolated beam, not a demonstrated +2.883% network effect. \(\sum\sqrt p\) decomposition is accounting, not causal separation of renewal from membership changes.
- **C’s common-floor ranking invariance is mathematically wrong:** \(9/8>10/10\), but \(9/28<10/30\). **R2’s cost calculation was also wrong:** 302 seconds on four workers is 0.3356 core-hours, not 0.0839.
- **B correctly finds the receive-pattern conflict but does not establish its proposed plateau as ITU-mandated.** The applicable lower limit is 2.04330°, not 2.498°. [ITU-R S.465-6, recommends 2](https://www.itu.int/dms_pubrec/itu-r/rec/s/R-REC-S.465-6-201001-I!!PDF-E.pdf)
- **EE correctly identifies legacy reward mismatch but overgeneralizes “training”; E identifies the actual consumers.** A fixed-price surplus has an exact ratio-improvement equivalence at the comparator’s exact ratio; neither arbitrary fixed prices nor summed difference rewards guarantee global ordering.
- **F’s freshness objection and E’s narrower “no demonstrated Stage-A collision” can both hold.** The first 100 Stage-C seeds demonstrably repeat earlier development; a complete training-world collision audit is still required. Missing local launch artifacts limit reproduction, not the independently checked historical receipt arithmetic.
- **HA’s proposed RANDOM≤BASE expectation is wrong as an acceptance criterion.** Only NULL equality is mandatory; random improvement is a reportable diagnostic. Forward-link adaptive power is also a legitimate architecture, so “all downlinks must use fixed RF” is too strong. [DVB implementation guide §4.1.2](https://dvb.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/a171-1_s2_guide.pdf)

## 2. Successor specification `V025-FIXED-EIRP-ACM`

1. **Identity and scope.** The primary candidate is a fixed-RF, fixed-boresight-EIRP forward link with ACM: directional EIRP still varies with antenna gain. The endpoint is full-buffer decodable PHY bits per modeled partial-payload joule. This is the paper’s system model.
2. **Operating point.** \(P_b=1.65\) W per active beam; \(p_{\rm sat}=1.65\,10^{5/10}=5.217758139\) W; \(\eta_{\max}=0.35\). Provenance: retained [benchmark coefficients](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/link_budget.py:175), not measured flight hardware; hardware attribution remains `VERIFY_SOURCE`. Reject 0.825 W as primary because its old half-cap recurrence budget has disappeared; it would imply 8.0103-dB back-off.
3. **PA.** \(P_{\rm PA}(p)=\sqrt{p\,p_{\rm sat}}/0.35\) for positive supported RF, with \(P_{\rm PA}(0)=0\). At \(P_b\), supply is 8.383317219 W and efficiency 0.196819. Back-off is already encoded; never subtract it again from emitted RF.
4. **Transmission.** Equal-airtime, full-band TDM among the assigned users of each beam. All users’ wanted signals and every interference contribution use the same actual beam RF. No segment-entry gain, recurrence, reset target or private-power/beam-max hybrid survives.
5. **Band/interference.** \(W=500/3\) MHz per colour; retain earth-fixed reuse-3, ideal colour orthogonality, full-buffer activity and the closed simulated interference population as benchmark assumptions. Count each active co-colour physical beam once; exclude the serving beam, not every beam with the same cell ID. [Current definitions](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/interference.py:384)
6. **Channel.** Retain the source-bound 20-GHz carrier, transmit pattern, 35-dBi terminal peak, 0.6-m dish, clear-sky loss tables, \(K=20\) dB and \(T_{\rm sys}=150+290(10^{1.2/10}-1)\) K; their interpretation remains benchmark-specific. Bind the complete constants/tables manifest to the cited checkout; no silent defaults.
7. **Antenna correction.** Compute S.465’s applicable \(\theta_{\min}=2.043298703^\circ\). Retain the existing clipped near-axis extrapolation as an explicitly named engineering approximation, not an ITU prescription; freeze a near-axis upper-envelope sensitivity separately. Fix numerical errors against an independent Bessel reference.
8. **ACM profile.** Use all 28 entries of EN 302 307-1 V1.4.1 Table 13, normal frames, no pilots; select the eligible mode with greatest efficiency. Choose roll-off \(\alpha=0.20\). For table entries \((e_m,g_m)\), use \(SE_m=e_m/1.20\) and \(\gamma_{m,\mathrm{dB}}=g_m+M-10\log_{10}(1.20)\). Freeze the transcribed table and its hash. [Table 13 and shaping rule](https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/302300_302399/30230701/01.04.01_60/en_30230701v010401p.pdf)
9. **Margin.** \(M=1.7\) dB is a prospective common receiver allowance taken from the reported upper 32APSK implementation loss; applying it across modes is this model’s choice. It is not a complete satellite distortion budget. [ITU-R BO.1784-1, attachment §1](https://www.itu.int/dms_pubrec/itu-r/rec/bo/R-REC-BO.1784-1-201612-I!!PDF-E.pdf)

| Profile anchor | Table efficiency, bit/symbol | Ideal threshold, dB | Model SE, bit/s/Hz | Model threshold, dB |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Lowest: QPSK 1/4 | 0.490243 | −2.35 | 0.408535833 | −1.441812460 |
| Alternative power-control target: 8PSK 2/3 | 1.980636 | 6.62 | 1.650530000 | 7.528187540 |
| Ceiling: 32APSK 9/10 | 4.453027 | 16.05 | 3.710855833 | 16.958187540 |

10. **Service.** For an allocated transmission, `served_PHY ⇔ SINR ≥ SINR_min`, with \(\mathrm{SINR}_{\min}=10^{-1.441812460/10}=0.7174947935\). Below it: zero decodable bits. NULL is unserved. Report time-weighted decoding availability and complete-service user-steps separately; this is not a minimum-throughput guarantee.
11. **Resolution order.** Fix assignments/airtime → radiate and compute joint interference → decode. Failed attempts retain RF energy/interference and their scheduled share; no retrospective pruning or bandwidth redistribution. Association legality is checked before execution; instantaneous decoding is checked afterward.
12. **Energy.** For interval pieces \(k\), \(E=\sum_k\delta_k\{\sum_{b\in\mathcal I_w}[z_{bk}(0.338+\max(P_{\rm idle},P_{\rm PA}(p_{bk})))+(1-z_{bk})P_{\rm idle}]+0.200N_{\rm active\,sat,k}+P_{\rm bus}\}\). An active satellite has at least one transmitting beam. Coefficients come from [the retained component model](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/link_budget.py:270); beam↔chain assignment is a benchmark assumption.
13. **Inventory/boundary.** \(\mathcal I_w\) is a manifest of physical NORAD/beam-chain identities fixed before actions and reused across arms and scored horizons; candidate slots do not define hardware. Primary \(P_{\rm idle}=0\), with ideal boundary switching; \(P_{\rm bus}=0\) denotes exclusion of bus, terminal, gateway and controller energy. Do not claim spacecraft EE or physically zero procedure energy.
14. **Standby sensitivity.** Use \(P_{\rm idle}=fP_{\rm PA}(1.65)\), \(f=35/420=1/12\), hence 0.698609768 W/chain. The cited ratio is from a ground Ka-band HPA’s mute/active prime-power ratings; transplanting it to this model is an engineering proxy, with flight applicability `VERIFY_SOURCE`. The active-floor clamp prevents “turn on at tiny RF to evade standby.” [CPI MKT-497, p.2](https://www.cpii.com/docs/datasheets/826/MKT-497%20SA49KOA_SB49KOA%2080%20W%20SSPA_SSPB.pdf)
15. **Handover.** No event-joule term. Primary interruption is zero; matrix interruption removes useful transmission time only, retaining scheduled RF draw. Separate same-satellite beam change, satellite change, reacquisition and initial entry; do not equate them automatically with RRC procedures.
16. **Interruption sensitivity.** Use 62/142 ms only as the conditional electronic-terminal/cell-handover scenarios recorded in [ADR-004:118](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/docs/decisions/ADR-004-payload-boundary-time-only-c2.md:118); live beam→cell/procedure applicability is `VERIFY_SOURCE`. Clip blackout overlap exactly once; initial entry/re-entry are separately logged, not silently assigned 142 ms.
17. **Clock.** Decisions remain every \(47×0.640=30.08\) s, from [native constants](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/constants.py:73). Primary integration covers \([t,t+30.08]\): the value at \(t\) plus the next 47 D2 boundary samples define 47 trapezoidal intervals; handle known event discontinuities explicitly. Never apply the new action to pre-\(t\) samples.
18. **Geometry/state.** Advance mobility causally within each interval; rebuild D2 history chronologically. Wire the existing benchmark 10° elevation parameter into the live visibility contract; retain D2’s 1100-km threshold, 50-km hysteresis, 1.280-s TTT and 300-km altitude floor with their [scenario provenance](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/d2.py:76).
19. **Actions.** Retain 28 association actions, four cached satellite identities, seven local cells and four-decision refresh; these are candidate restrictions, not minimum residence. NO_OP remains legal exactly for an empty mask. Joint SINR is not an independently guaranteeable per-user legality mask; outage does not authorize hidden rescue.
20. **Observable information.** Policy inputs use current nominal geometry, D2 state and explicitly timestamped lagged telemetry. Remove independent “current CSI” noise and the false elevation fallback. Realized fading stays hidden from deployment selectors; forecasts use ephemeris/current motion assumptions, not future random realizations.
21. **Calibration.** Freeze a deterministic nominal-greedy reference policy and disjoint TRAIN calibration worlds before probes. For each setting, \(\eta_{\rm ref}=B_{\rm ref}/E_{\rm ref}\), \(\lambda=\eta_{\rm ref}\), and \(\kappa=B_{\rm ref}/(U T_{\rm ref})\), including outage opportunities. Require positive calibration totals; freeze once. Rule provenance: [calibration.py:70](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/runtime/ee_axis_calibration.py:70), prospectively corrected here.
22. **Reward.** The auditable EE core is \(R_t=B_t-\eta_{\rm ref}E_t\), using exactly the endpoint’s bits and energy, including the setting’s interruption/standby treatment. Retain and sum this core independently of normalization, credit assignment and QoS shaping.
23. **Φ/κ mapping.** Optimize normalized core plus explicitly separate QoS terms. Retain \(\Phi_1=0.5,\Phi_2=1.0\) as benchmark event preferences from [action_contract.py:408](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/src/mcrl/env/action_contract.py:408), never as joules/seconds; use the same event ledger and coefficients in all compared teachers/selectors. Report EE, availability, rate tails, outage runs and handovers as co-primary outcomes.
24. **Persistence penalty.** Separate scale κ from the service-loss coefficient: retain one κ of disutility per lost projected offset as a stated design preference, report it separately, and recalibrate κ. This preserves the old normalized loss magnitude without claiming it is physical energy.
25. **C1.** Replace focal-bit-only opening labels with full-network difference surplus against the declared default action, plus the same explicit QoS difference. Do not claim that summing user difference rewards reconstructs the global core.
26. **C2 historical replacements.** Replace `previous_recurrence_power` with incumbent nominal decoding margin; gain-to-entry ratio with forecast SE trend; `segment_age` with remaining D2/visibility time. Retain `missing_incumbent` as a service-state flag; add refresh phase. Apply this migration to Q1’s historical tail as well as actual Q2 consumers.
27. **C2 forecast schema.** For every action include background occupancy, beam/satellite activation, current decoding margin, remaining visibility and refresh phase; for each of three future offsets include validity, survival, minimum decoding margin and mean ACM SE. Alternative power-control settings additionally carry required-power/cap margin. Freeze units, shapes and calibration-based normalization under new schema hashes.
28. **C2 projection.** Preserve three-offset absorbing loss only inside the persistence forecast; live recovery remains possible. Hold background associations, recompute geometry, radiation, interference and energy under the selected physics. Charge a failed attempt before terminating persistence. Forecast length is physical and independent of scored-horizon truncation.
29. **C3.** A set-level consolidation coordinator operates on complete proposals, scores whole configurations with the same system objective/QoS terms, and commits atomically. BASE is always present and wins ties. Include unilateral proposals and genuine multi-user evacuations; count actual changed users, not catalogue labels. S0 uses deployable nominal physics; learned S3 must additionally beat matched S0.
30. **Learning contract.** Regenerate all physics-dependent targets, datasets, caches and normalizers. Retain explicit outage transitions. Any retrained MODQN comparator uses a consistent scalar objective/bootstrap and isolated evaluation RNG. Old checkpoints are transfer controls only.
31. **History seal.** Preserve old BASE/Q1/Q2/Q3 checkpoints, λ/κ, recurrence state/OPS-3 schemas, source corpora, attempt-4 bindings, R7, F1, E1, S0, C3-S v1/FULL/LITE, old Stage-C ladders, anchor/churn diagnostics and nine-arm matrices unchanged. None becomes successor evidence by relabelling.

## 3. Known-answer test suite

Use independent scalar calculations and synthetic worlds; parity between two calls to the production helper is insufficient. Constants below are either §2 values or explicitly synthetic fixture values.

1. **Joules/deduplication:** one active beam on one satellite for 30.08 s costs \((8.383317219+0.338+0.200)30.08=268.353221940\) J; adding a second user adds no PA/circuit charge.
2. **Bits:** synthetic \(W=12\) Hz, two equal-airtime users, two seconds, both at 8PSK 2/3: 19.80636 bits/user and 39.61272 total. Apply airtime, roll-off and duration exactly once.
3. **Handover-count invariance:** identical radiation, activation and geometry histories with different bookkeeping/event counts have identical E. Interruption changes B only. Actual moves to different physical states need not preserve energy in power-control alternatives.
4. **Lit-beam monotonicity:** at fixed \(P_b\), activating another beam on an already active satellite adds \((8.721317219-P_{\rm idle})\delta\) J; activating its first satellite beam also adds \(0.200\delta\).
5. **SE ceiling/table:** arbitrarily high SINR yields at most 3.710855833 bit/s/Hz; dominated table entries never displace a higher-efficiency eligible mode.
6. **Service boundary:** at \(\gamma_{\min}\), an allocated sample is served; the adjacent representable value below is not. Test NULL, partial-interval outage and nominal-pass/realized-fail separately.
7. **Pooling discriminator:** \((B,E)=(10,1),(10,9)\) gives pooled EE 2, versus mean ratios \(5.555\ldots\); duplication leaves pooled EE unchanged.
8. **Reward–endpoint identity:** for any fixed trajectory, \(\sum_tR_t=\sum_tB_t-\eta_{\rm ref}\sum_tE_t\). With \((B_{\rm ref},E_{\rm ref})=(100,10)\), the surplus-change sign matches the EE-change sign exactly.
9. **NULL≡BASE:** run the complete coordinator path but commit BASE; require identical actions, state, random keys, raw physics, B, E and service traces, excluding timing metadata.
10. **CRN matching:** world/user/NORAD/absolute-time/channel-component keys are identical across arms/settings; candidate reordering, duplicate evaluation and renewal do not reroll fading.
11. **RF consistency:** two equal-channel users on one fixed-RF beam both use 1.65 W in wanted fields; victims and PA accounting see that same transmission.
12. **TDM PA averaging:** equal 0.825/1.65-W slots give mean PA supply 7.155608836 W, not \(P_{\rm PA}(1.2375)=7.260165679\) W; overlapping beams follow the same slot clock.
13. **FDM conservation:** two equal subbands each receive \(W/2\), their own \(kTW/2\), consistent overlapping interference and RF allocations summing to at most 1.65 W.
14. **Memoryless control:** doubling isolated nominal channel gain halves required unsaturated RF; history changes do nothing. Exercise coupled convergence, cap binding, lower-MODCOD operation and solver failure without reused prior power.
15. **Standby inventory:** a dark chain contributes exactly \(0.698609768\delta\) J in the cited-proxy setting; changing candidate enumeration changes nothing. Tiny active RF cannot evade the standby floor.
16. **Interference/failed attempts:** synthetic wanted 10, intra 2, inter 3, off-colour 4 yields interference 5. A decoding failure does not remove its attempted beam from anyone else’s interference or energy.
17. **Time/causality:** \(R(t)=t\) over 30.08 s integrates to 452.4032 bits with trapezoidal samples; a terminal snapshot gives 904.8064. An action at \(t\) cannot change any pre-\(t\) receipt.
18. **D2/visibility/dwell:** latched 1000→1100-km history remains latched; short band-only initialization must not impersonate it. A 5° link fails the wired 10° mask; \(378/(4×30.08)=3.141\) refresh periods.
19. **Initialization/RNG:** world B alone or after A has identical initial state; changing scored horizon preserves initial state/common prefix; an evaluation call cannot alter the next training world.
20. **C2:** changing only segment-entry gain changes no successor feature/target. A projected loss remains absorbing in that forecast; live reconnection works. Verify three physical offsets even near the scoring endpoint.
21. **System externality:** focal +1 bit, nonfocal −10 bits, ΔE=0 gives immediate teacher surplus −9, not +1; a two-user evacuation must be distinguishable from singleton aliases.
22. **Independent reconstruction/mutations:** detect doubled bits, repeated PA/baseband charges, altered physical identities, stale source hashes and state pollution from counterfactual evaluation.
23. **Statistical/control discriminators:** exercise the common-floor ranking reversal above, missing all-neutral factorial cell, reused world IDs and arbitrarily tiny deterministic “passes”; none may silently become confirmatory support.
24. **Channel arithmetic:** independently check FSPL doubling-distance loss, \(kT_{\rm sys}W≈5.5754×10^{-13}\) W, S.465’s 2.04330° branch and Bessel accuracy; distinguish median-normalized shadow from unit-mean Rician power.
25. **Outage/deployment contract:** retain zero-bit/zero-energy dark steps in opportunities; an all-dark panel has explicit undefined EE, not dropped rows. All-false masks execute NO_OP; injected deadline misses execute the frozen BASE fallback.

## 4. Re-run plan with stop rules and cost

**Implementation — Codex.** Build one versioned radiation/rate/energy engine used by execution, teachers, forecasts and nominal scoring; then repair clock/D2 initialization, observation semantics, masks/NO_OP, C2 schemas, legacy comparator training and receipt reconstruction. Bind constants, model versions, complete state, source hashes and world manifests before producing outcomes. Budget approximately **3–5 engineering days**, including the two power-control alternatives; this is an estimate, not a completed implementation.

**Reuse Track B’s runner and shared-tape contract.** Recover and authenticate the server’s lever-matrix implementation; it was not located in the supplied checkouts. The available [oracle-marginal runner](/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover-e1/.scratch/multi-catfish-v023-c3-probe-oracle-marginals/oracle_marginals.py:1) supplies scaffolding, but its old targets, checkpoints and max-power physics must be replaced. L1 `RATE_TARGET_SUM_POWER` is the FDM branch below; TDM requires a distinct airtime-consistent implementation.

